    "title": "Empowering Local Farmers: DOLE and LGU Kawayan Launch WODP Training",
    "author": "LGU Kawayan",
    "date": "May 8, 2026",
    "image": "/news/09.jpg",
    "body": "In a significant move to bolster the organizational strength of the local agricultural sector, the Department of Labor and Employment (DOLE) Biliran Field Office, in close coordination with the Local Government Unit (LGU) of Kawayan, recently spearheaded a Workers Organization Development Program (WODP) Capability Training. This initiative targeted the leaders and members of three prominent Irrigators Associations within the municipality, reflecting a shared commitment to improving the socio-economic welfare of rural workers. By providing specialized training, the program aims to transform these associations into more resilient and self-sustaining entities.\n\nThe comprehensive training module was designed to equip participants with a versatile skill set essential for modern organizational management. Key topics included an in-depth Overview of the WODP, a refresher on General Labor Standards, and critical discussions on Occupational Safety and Health (OSH). Furthermore, a dedicated segment on Leadership Development was integrated to inspire officers to lead with integrity and strategic vision. This holistic approach ensures that members are not only aware of their rights and safety in the workplace but are also prepared to manage their associations with professional excellence.\n\nThe activity saw active participation from three vital grassroots organizations: the Kawayan-Anas-Ungale Farmers Irrigators Association (KANUFIA), the Tucdao Anas Farmers Irrigators Association, Inc. (TAFIA), and the Mapuyo Kawayan Biliran Farmers Irrigators Association, Inc. (MAKABFIA). These groups represent the backbone of Kawayan’s farming community, and their involvement underscores the local government's strategy of empowering the agricultural labor force. Through this training, the associations are expected to improve their internal governance and increase their capacity to advocate for the needs of their members.\n\nThe event was graced by key officials who provided expert guidance and support throughout the sessions. Leading the delegation was Mr. Francisco A. Segovia, Jr., OIC-Head of the DOLE Biliran Field Office, alongside Ms. Flordelis O. Ceniza, Senior Labor and Employment Officer, and their dedicated staff. Representing the municipal interests and ensuring local coordination was Mr. John Carlo D. Dugan, Labor and Employment Officer III of LGU Kawayan. Their presence reaffirmed the strong partnership between national agencies and local government units in delivering impactful social services.\n\nUltimately, the WODP Capability Training serves as a catalyst for sustainable development in Biliran's agricultural landscape. By fostering a culture of safety, legal compliance, and effective leadership, DOLE and LGU Kawayan are laying the groundwork for more robust workers’ organizations. As these Irrigators Associations apply their newfound knowledge to their daily operations, they contribute to a more organized, informed, and empowered farming sector, capable of driving long-term economic growth for the entire municipality."
